### Client Ops & Campaign Manager -- manual verification requests
### Requires the VS Code "REST Client" extension. Run `npm run db:setup` first so the seeded
### users/clients/campaigns/tasks below exist. Each block is annotated with the expected result --
### this is the "requests.http" companion referenced in the README (see also src/database/seeders
### for exactly what data each seeded user/permission/assignment looks like).

@baseUrl = http://localhost:4000

### ============================================================================
### AUTH -- login as every seeded role, capture access tokens for use below
### ============================================================================

### Login: SuperAdmin
# @name loginSuperadmin
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "superadmin@example.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### Login: Manager1 (paid_media, assigned to Acme Sportswear / client_id=1)
# @name loginManager1
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "manager1@example.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### Login: Employee1 (paid_media, reports to Manager1)
# @name loginEmployee1
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "employee1@example.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### Login: Manager2 (storefront_craft, assigned to Nova Skincare / client_id=2)
# @name loginManager2
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "manager2@example.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### Login: Employee2 (storefront_craft, reports to Manager2)
# @name loginEmployee2
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "employee2@example.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### Login: Client1 (read-only, owns Acme Sportswear / client_id=1)
# @name loginClient1
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "client1@example.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### Login: Client2 (read-only, owns Nova Skincare / client_id=2)
# @name loginClient2
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "client2@example.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### Login with wrong password -- expect 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "manager1@example.com",
  "password": "wrong-password"
}

### Hit a protected route with no Authorization header -- expect 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout


### ============================================================================
### CLIENT-ACCESS CASCADE -- clients list differs per role, no separate table for employees
### ============================================================================

### SuperAdmin: list clients -- expect ALL (Acme + Nova)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/clients
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Manager1: list clients -- expect only Acme (their client_assignments row)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/clients
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Employee1: list clients -- expect only Acme too, resolved via manager1's assignment
### (no separate assignment row exists for employees -- this is the cascade in action)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/clients
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Client1: list clients -- expect only their own (Acme)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/clients
Authorization: Bearer {{loginClient1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### SuperAdmin: create a new client -- expect 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/clients
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "company_name": "Test Client Co"
}

### Manager1: try to create a client -- expect 403 (no department grants client:create)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/clients
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "company_name": "Unauthorized Client"
}

### Client2: read client 1 (Acme, not theirs) -- expect 403 from clientScope, not permission
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/clients/1
Authorization: Bearer {{loginClient2.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Client1: read client 1 (their own) -- expect 200 (client role bypasses the permission table
### for reads, see permission.middleware.js -- clients aren't part of the department system)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/clients/1
Authorization: Bearer {{loginClient1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Nested read alias: Employee1 views Acme's campaigns via /clients/:id/campaigns
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/clients/1/campaigns
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee1.response.body.$.accessToken}}


### ============================================================================
### CAMPAIGNS -- permission + client-scope both enforced, checked as separate steps
### ============================================================================

### SuperAdmin: list all campaigns (both clients)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/campaigns
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Manager1: list campaigns -- expect only Acme's
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/campaigns
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Manager1: create a campaign for client_id=1 (assigned to them) -- expect 201
# @name createCampaign
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/campaigns
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_id": 1,
  "channel": "tiktok",
  "name": "New TikTok push",
  "budget": 800
}

### Manager1: create a campaign for client_id=2 (NOT assigned to them) -- expect 403
### Demonstrates: holding campaign:create generally doesn't grant access to every client.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/campaigns
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_id": 2,
  "channel": "google",
  "name": "Unauthorized attempt",
  "budget": 100
}

### Client1: try to create a campaign -- expect 403 (clients are read-only, always)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/campaigns
Authorization: Bearer {{loginClient1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_id": 1,
  "channel": "meta",
  "name": "Client attempt"
}

### Employee1: update campaign 1 (paid_media dept default includes campaign:update) -- expect 200
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/campaigns/1
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "paused"
}

### Manager1: soft-delete the campaign created above -- expect 204
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/campaigns/{{createCampaign.response.body.$.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### SuperAdmin: list campaigns again -- the deleted one is gone from results (soft delete, not
### a real row removal -- see README's "no hard deletes" design decision)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/campaigns
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}


### ============================================================================
### TASKS -- special case: PATCH allowed if self-assigned OR holds task:update
### ============================================================================

### Employee1: list tasks -- expect Acme's tasks, resolved via manager1's client assignment
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Employee1: update task 1 (assigned to them) -- expect 200 via the self-assign branch
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/1
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "in_progress"
}

### Manager1: update task 1 (NOT assigned to them, but holds task:update) -- expect 200 via the
### permission branch -- clientScope still applies to both branches regardless of which grants access.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/1
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "open"
}

### Employee2: try to update task 1 (Acme's, not their client) -- expect 403 from clientScope
### (blocked before the self-assign/permission branch is even reached)
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/1
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee2.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "done"
}


### ============================================================================
### STOREFRONTS / MARKETPLACE ACCOUNTS -- same permission + clientScope pattern as campaigns
### ============================================================================

### Manager2: create a storefront for Nova Skincare (client_id=2, assigned to them) -- expect 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/storefronts
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager2.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_id": 2,
  "platform": "shopify",
  "url": "https://nova-skincare.myshopify.com"
}

### SuperAdmin: create a marketplace account for Acme (client_id=1) -- expect 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/marketplace-accounts
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_id": 1,
  "platform": "amazon"
}


### ============================================================================
### PERMISSION DELEGATION -- SuperAdmin->Manager and Manager->Employee, same shared endpoint
### ============================================================================

### SuperAdmin grants Manager2 permission_id=1 (client:create) -- expect 204
### (SuperAdmin bypasses the additive department/grant system entirely -- see canGrantPermission.js)
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/users/4/permissions
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "permission_id": 1
}

### Manager1 tries to grant a permission to Manager2 (a peer manager) -- expect 403
### Demonstrates: a Manager can never alter another Manager's permissions, full stop.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/users/4/permissions
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "permission_id": 20
}

### Manager1 tries to grant Employee1 a permission Manager1 doesn't hold (client:create) -- expect 403
### Demonstrates the core delegation rule: can't grant what you don't yourself hold.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/users/3/permissions
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "permission_id": 1
}

### Manager2 (now holds client:create from the grant above) grants it to Employee2 -- expect 204
### Demonstrates Manager->Employee delegation using the *same* canGrantPermission function.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/users/5/permissions
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager2.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "permission_id": 1
}


### ============================================================================
### PERMISSION REVOCATION -- per-user overrides, see README §5.8 and the design spec at
### docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-13-permission-revocation-design.md
### ============================================================================

### Check Employee1's current permission status -- expect campaign CRUD + client:read as
### "department", task:delete as "grant"
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/users/3/permissions
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Manager1 revokes campaign:delete (permission_id=16) from Employee1 -- a department default,
### not something Manager1 needs to hold themselves (revocation is unrestricted) -- expect 204
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/users/3/permissions/16
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Employee1 tries to actually delete a campaign now -- expect 403 (this is real enforcement,
### not just a display change: getEffectivePermissions genuinely excludes the revoked permission)
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/campaigns/1
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Manager1 un-revokes it (PATCH = "make this held") -- expect 204, and the permission's `source`
### reverts to "department", not "grant" -- this restores the default, it isn't a fresh grant
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/users/3/permissions
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "permission_id": 16
}

### SuperAdmin revokes campaign:delete from Manager1 themselves -- expect 204
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/users/2/permissions/16
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Manager1 (who no longer holds campaign:delete) still revokes it from Employee1 anyway --
### expect 204. Demonstrates: revocation is unrestricted, unlike granting.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/users/3/permissions/16
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### For contrast: Manager1 (still without campaign:delete) tries to grant client:create (a
### permission that is NOT a paid_media department default) to Employee1 -- expect 403. Granting
### is still bounded by canGrantPermission; only revoking is unrestricted.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/users/3/permissions
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "permission_id": 1
}

### Manager1 tries to revoke something from Manager2 (a peer manager) -- expect 403 (ownership
### rule still applies to revocation, same as granting)
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/users/4/permissions/16
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}


### ============================================================================
### USER DEACTIVATION CASCADE -- deactivating a Manager cascades to their Employees
### ============================================================================

### Employee2: confirm access works before deactivation
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee2.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### SuperAdmin deactivates Manager2 -- expect Employee2 to be cascaded to is_active=false too,
### and both to have their sessions revoked immediately (see users.service.js#setActiveStatus)
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/users/4/status
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "is_active": false
}

### Employee2's still-valid (< 15 min old) access token is rejected immediately -- expect 401
### (auth.middleware re-checks is_active live on every request, not just on refresh)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee2.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Employee2 tries to log in again while inactive -- expect 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "employee2@example.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### Cleanup: reactivate Manager2 (reactivation is never auto-cascaded -- must be explicit per user)
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/users/4/status
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "is_active": true
}

### Cleanup: reactivate Employee2 too
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/users/5/status
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "is_active": true
}


### ============================================================================
### MANAGERS / CLIENT ASSIGNMENT
### ============================================================================

### SuperAdmin assigns Nova Skincare (client_id=2) to Manager1 as a second client -- expect 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/managers/2/clients
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_id": 2
}

### Manager1 tries to assign clients themselves -- expect 403 (SuperAdmin only)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/managers/2/clients
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_id": 2
}


### ============================================================================
### DEPARTMENTS / AUDIT LOGS
### ============================================================================

### Any authenticated staff role can list departments (dropdown data)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/departments
Authorization: Bearer {{loginEmployee1.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Non-SuperAdmin tries to create a department -- expect 403
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/departments
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "new_dept"
}

### SuperAdmin views the audit log -- expect every mutation above to show up here
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/audit-logs
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Manager1 tries to view the audit log -- expect 403 (SuperAdmin only)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/audit-logs
Authorization: Bearer {{loginManager1.response.body.$.accessToken}}


### ============================================================================
### REFRESH / LOGOUT
### ============================================================================

### Refresh using the cookie set by the SuperAdmin login above -- expect a rotated token pair
### (REST Client persists cookies per host automatically within a session)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/refresh

### Logout -- expect 204, and the refresh token to be revoked (a subsequent refresh 401s)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{loginSuperadmin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
